#!/bin/sh
# Pre-flight gate for dispatching a writing agent (i-scripts-dispatch-dispatch-wrappe).
#
#   scripts/dispatch i-foo --files "src/x.ts tests/x.test.ts" --brief "one-line task"
#
# Exit 0 means SAFE TO DISPATCH; anything else names the hazard. It does not
# spawn the agent — the Agent tool does that — it refuses the four dispatch
# mistakes that have each burned a real agent run:
#
#   1. NON-ROOT CWD. A worktree is created in whatever repo the dispatching
#      session's cwd belongs to; parked inside the nested planning-docs repo,
#      agents get a docs worktree with no janus-bridge/ or package.json.
#      Happened three times before scripts/tracker removed the cd; this checks
#      the caller too.
#   2. UNCOMMITTED WORK INSIDE THE AGENT'S OWN FILE SCOPE. worktree.baseRef is
#      "head" (2026-08-09), so an agent inherits local HEAD — unpushed commits
#      ARE visible and nothing has to be pushed before dispatching. What is
#      still invisible is uncommitted edits, so handing an agent a file you are
#      mid-edit in means it works from the committed version and its result
#      silently drops your changes. Only the declared scope is checked; a dirty
#      tree elsewhere is none of this agent's business.
#   3. ALREADY-LANDED WORK. If git log already mentions the issue id, the task
#      may be partly or fully done (i-verify-retempto sat unflipped for a day
#      after its flag landed). The audit comes before the dispatch.
#   4. UNRECORDED BRIEFS. The brief + file scope are appended to the tracker
#      notes log in the same act, so any session can see what was handed out
#      and scopes can be checked for overlap before agents collide in a file.
#
# THE SCOPE LIST IS A DENYLIST, NOT AN ALLOWLIST
# (i-dispatch-declares-what-is-claimed-by-others-not). --files used to declare
# the files the agent MAY write: a prediction made at the moment of least
# information, by the one party who has not read the code, and correcting it
# 20 minutes in costs a round trip. The dispatcher does not know what the agent
# will need. It knows one thing that is true and small: which files OTHER
# agents are holding right now, because it dispatched them. So the ENFORCED,
# PASTED thing is now the exclusion set, and --files stays as an advisory
# prediction that is still recorded for the audit trail.
#
# All three recorded scope failures were exclusions with nothing behind them:
#   i-commune-s-failed-controller-turn  could not reach src/shared/types.ts, so
#       a Go writer shipped an undeclared event kind — but types.ts is
#       append-only and tested as such, so it never needed to be off-limits.
#   i-dist-version-stamp                shipped a janusVersion wire field with
#       no producer because cmd/janus-bridge was outside scope. Nobody else was
#       in that file; it was excluded by omission.
#   i-a-dispatched-agent-cannot-read-the-tracker-its  had its own brand-new
#       changelog.d fragment refused by `handoff --lift`, purely because the
#       scope line had been narrowed to get past a gate.
#
# THE CLAIM SET NEEDS NO NEW STATE. This script already writes `DISPATCH <id> —
# scope: …` to the notes log and scripts/handoff already records the release,
# so the live claims are derivable from the log: an issue whose last event is a
# DISPATCH holds its scope, a HANDOFF releases it, and a claim by the id being
# dispatched now is not a claim against this agent. Unreachable tracker degrades
# to "unavailable" and the context file tells the agent to treat the advisory
# list as hard — an unreadable claim log is not an empty one.
#
# COLLISION DETECTION STAYS AT THE LAND STEP. `handoff --lift` compares what the
# worker changed against the recorded denylist and refuses a claimed file just
# as hard as it used to refuse an out-of-allowlist one; a file outside --files
# that nobody claimed is now reported for the lander to read, not refused.
#
# IT ALSO WRITES THE AGENT'S CONTEXT FILE (i-a-dispatched-agent-cannot-read-the-tracker-its).
# Everything this script already knew was printed to the DISPATCHER's terminal
# and reached the agent only if a human retyped it. Measured over 224 subagent
# transcripts / 152 worktree agents: 12 of 152 (8%) ever opened .claude/CLAUDE.md
# — 15 of the 40 whose brief said to read it — while 67 of the 70 told to write
# HANDOFF.md did (96%) and ~0 of the 82 not told did. Compliance tracks what the
# brief PASTES, not what it points at. 60 of 152 reached for planning-docs/ or
# scripts/tracker anyway: 322 calls, 49 hard failures. One run burned ~75k tokens
# on work that had already landed, because nobody checked git log for the id.
# So the issue JSON, the carry-forward lines, `git log --grep`, the scope, the
# brief and .claude/CLAUDE.md verbatim are now WRITTEN to a file, and the brief
# block's first line is the absolute path to it.
#
# WHY baseRef IS "head" AND WHY THAT IS SAFE NOW. It was "fresh" (origin/main)
# because "head" resolves against whatever repo the session's cwd belongs to,
# and sessions kept drifting into the nested planning-docs repo — every dispatch
# then died with `fatal: invalid reference: <a planning-docs SHA>`. Both halves
# of that drift are now mechanically closed: scripts/tracker runs in a subshell
# so the cwd cannot move, and check 1 below refuses a non-root cwd outright.
# With "fresh", every dispatch required a push first, which serialised landing
# across concurrent sessions for no safety benefit — agents are isolated by
# their worktree, not by their base commit.
#
# --anyway skips check 3 only (the git-log hits are printed regardless); checks
# 1 and 2 have no override, because there is no correct dispatch from a wrong
# cwd or over your own uncommitted edits.
set -e

usage() { echo "usage: scripts/dispatch <issue-id> --files \"<paths>\" --brief \"<text>\" [--anyway] [--dry-run]" >&2; exit 2; }

id=""; files=""; brief=""; anyway=0; dry=0
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --files) files="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --brief) brief="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --anyway) anyway=1; shift ;;
    # Run every check and print the brief block, but write nothing to the log.
    # Added after a smoke test of this script left a phantom `DISPATCH
    # i-probe-smoke` in the shared append-only log — a note that can be
    # annotated but never removed, and that reads to any later session as an
    # agent someone forgot to land. scripts/handoff already had --dry-run for
    # exactly this reason; the script that writes FIRST did not.
    --dry-run) dry=1; shift ;;
    -*) usage ;;
    *) [ -n "$id" ] && usage; id="$1"; shift ;;
  esac
done
[ -n "$id" ] && [ -n "$files" ] && [ -n "$brief" ] || usage

root=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)

if [ "$(pwd -P)" != "$root" ]; then
  echo "dispatch: REFUSED — cwd is $(pwd -P), not the repo root ($root)." >&2
  echo "  Worktrees are created in the repo the dispatching session's cwd is in." >&2
  echo "  Run:  cd $root" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# The handoff gate is a commit-msg hook and nothing else — this project's agents
# are dispatched, landed and verified locally, so there is no CI copy to fall
# back on (scripts/handoff-guard.sh explains why one is neither wanted nor
# possible). That makes an uninstalled hook a silent total loss of the gate, in a
# checkout that looks identical to one that has it. Checked HERE because dispatch
# is the last moment before the only activity the gate protects, and because
# commit-msg is NEWER than pre-commit: a session that installed hooks before
# 2026-08-09 has the changelog hook, no handoff hook, and no reason to suspect it.
#
# BOTH halves are checked, because either one missing kills the gate silently
# and the first version of this check only tested the shim. The installed shim
# execs "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/scripts/git-hooks/commit-msg" and
# self-disables with `[ -x "$HOOK" ] || exit 0` when that file is absent. The
# hooks directory is SHARED by every worktree of this repo, but the file the
# shim resolves is per-worktree — so in a worktree whose checkout predates the
# hook (verified in browser-wrap-bridge on feat/bridge-v1) the shim is present,
# the gate is dead, and checking only the shim reports OK.
hooks=$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks)
case "$hooks" in /*) ;; *) hooks="$root/$hooks" ;; esac
if [ ! -x "$hooks/commit-msg" ]; then
  echo "dispatch: REFUSED — no commit-msg shim in $hooks." >&2
  echo "  Without it nothing requires a handoff when you land this agent, and the" >&2
  echo "  only record of what it found and did not fix dies with its worktree." >&2
  echo "  Run:  npm run hooks:install" >&2
  exit 1
fi
if [ ! -x "$root/scripts/git-hooks/commit-msg" ]; then
  echo "dispatch: REFUSED — the commit-msg shim is installed but this checkout has" >&2
  echo "  no scripts/git-hooks/commit-msg, so the shim exits 0 and the gate is dead." >&2
  echo "  $root is on a commit older than the hook." >&2
  echo "  Merge or rebase onto a commit that has it, or land this work from the" >&2
  echo "  main checkout instead." >&2
  exit 1
fi

branch=$(git branch --show-current)
head=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)
echo "dispatch: agent will branch from $branch @ $head (baseRef: head)."

# Uncommitted edits are invisible inside the worktree. Only a collision with the
# agent's OWN declared scope is a hazard, so that is all this refuses on — a
# dirty tree elsewhere is normal and none of this agent's business.
dirty=$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $NF}')
clash=""
for f in $files; do
  # Strip the annotations a scope line carries: "path/(own)", "path/*.md", "dir/".
  pat=$(echo "$f" | sed 's|(own)$||; s|/\*.*$||; s|/$||')
  [ -n "$pat" ] || continue
  for d in $dirty; do
    case "$d" in $pat|$pat/*) clash="$clash $d" ;; esac
  done
done
if [ -n "$clash" ]; then
  echo "dispatch: REFUSED — you have uncommitted edits inside the agent's own file scope:" >&2
  for c in $clash; do echo "    $c" >&2; done
  echo "  The agent inherits committed HEAD, so it would work from the old version" >&2
  echo "  and its result would silently drop these edits. Commit them first." >&2
  exit 1
fi

hits=$(git log --oneline --grep="$id" | head -5)
if [ -n "$hits" ]; then
  echo "dispatch: git log already mentions $id:" >&2
  echo "$hits" >&2
  if [ "$anyway" != "1" ]; then
    echo "  Audit what landed before briefing (scripts/tracker show $id), then rerun with --anyway." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DENYLIST — what OTHER agents are holding right now
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derived from the shared notes log, which already carries both halves: this
# script writes `DISPATCH <id> — scope: …` and scripts/handoff writes
# `HANDOFF <id> — … | scope released: …`. Last event per id wins, so a
# re-dispatched issue reclaims its files and a handed-off one releases them.
# No new state, no registry file, nothing to keep in sync.
#
# A CLAIM EXPIRES AFTER 24h (DISPATCH_CLAIM_HOURS), and changelog.d/ is never
# claimable. Both were found by running the parser against the REAL notes log
# rather than a fixture: it returned nine live claims, four of them smoke tests
# of this script that can never be handed off, and two of them listing
# changelog.d/ — which every agent writes its own file in, by design. See the
# python below for the reasoning; the fixtures could not have shown either.
#
# THREE STATES, and they are not the same. A real list is enforced at lift; an
# empty list (`(none)`) means the log was read and nobody holds anything, so
# every file is the agent's; `(unavailable)` means the log could not be read at
# all, and that degrades to the OLD allowlist behaviour on both ends — the
# context file tells the agent to treat --files as hard, and handoff falls back
# to refusing anything outside it. An unreadable claim log is not an empty one,
# and the fail-open version of this would hand every concurrent agent a blank
# cheque on the exact round the tracker is down.
claims=""; deny=""; claim_lines=""; deny_ok=0
if claims_raw=$("$root/scripts/tracker" notes 2>/dev/null); then
  deny_ok=1
  claims=$(printf '%s\n' "$claims_raw" | python3 -c '
import datetime, os, re, sys
me = sys.argv[1]

# A CLAIM EXPIRES. The notes log is append-only with no delete, so a dispatch
# that was never handed off holds its files forever — and running this parser
# against the real log the day it was written found NINE live claims, four of
# them (i-probe, i-probe-smoke, i-scratch-dispatch-probe, i-scratch-claim-probe)
# smoke tests of this very script, which can never be handed off. That is the
# incident --dry-run was added for, seen from the other end. Without a window
# the denylist only grows, and its end state is an exclusion set that denies
# everything — the allowlist again, with worse ergonomics. A round is hours; 24
# is generous.
try:
    hours = float(os.environ.get("DISPATCH_CLAIM_HOURS", "24"))
except ValueError:
    hours = 24.0
cutoff = datetime.datetime.now() - datetime.timedelta(hours=hours)

# PATHS THAT CANNOT BE CONTENDED, so a claim on one is not a wall. changelog.d/
# is one file per entry BY DESIGN — precisely so two agents writing entries
# touch two different files — and every agent writes its own. Denying it would
# reproduce the 2026-08-26 failure this whole inversion exists to fix, through
# the other door: on the real log that day two live claims listed changelog.d/
# and would have walled off every incoming agent from its own fragment.
NEVER_CLAIMED = ("changelog.d",)

def ts_of(line):
    m = re.match(r"\s*(\d{4}-\d\d-\d\d)T(\d\d:\d\d:\d\d)", line)
    if not m:
        return None
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.strptime(m.group(1) + " " + m.group(2), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
    except ValueError:
        return None

def strip(p):
    p = re.sub(r"\(own\)$", "", p)
    p = re.sub(r"/\*.*$", "", p)
    return p.rstrip("/")

# THE VERB IS ANCHORED TO THE NOTE PREFIX, NOT SEARCHED FOR ANYWHERE IN THE
# LINE, and both verbs are read from ONE match so their order cannot matter.
# A free-text search found DISPATCH inside a HANDOFF note and the mistake was
# not theoretical - it shipped and was caught by using it, 40 minutes after it
# landed. The worker on i-dispatch-declares-what-is-claimed-by-others-not
# documented the note format in its own HANDOFF.md assumed: line:
#     "<ts> [<id>]  DISPATCH <id> - scope: ... - brief: ..."
# --verified lifted that text into the note verbatim. The old loop matched the
# DISPATCH branch inside that HANDOFF line, hit `continue`, and never reached
# the release - so BOTH issues landed that day went on holding scripts/dispatch
# and scripts/handoff after being handed off, and a phantom claim was registered
# under the literal id "<id>". A note that describes the log format must not be
# read as an entry in it.
VERB = re.compile(r"(?:^|\]\s+|note:\s+)(DISPATCH|HANDOFF)\s+(\S+)\s+—")

live, order = {}, []
for line in sys.stdin:
    m = VERB.search(line)
    if not m:
        continue
    verb, iid = m.group(1), m.group(2)
    if verb == "HANDOFF":
        live.pop(iid, None)
        continue
    # From the end of the ID group, not the end of the whole match: VERB
    # consumes the em dash, so searching the remainder for one finds nothing.
    scope_m = re.search(r"—\s+scope:\s*(.*?)\s+—\s+brief:", line[m.end(2):])
    if not scope_m:
        continue
    when = ts_of(line)
    # An unparseable timestamp counts as live: failing safe here costs a
    # false wall, failing open costs a clobber.
    if when is not None and when < cutoff:
        live.pop(iid, None)
        continue
    live[iid] = scope_m.group(1).strip()
    if iid not in order:
        order.append(iid)

paths = []
for iid in order:
    # A claim by the id being dispatched now is this agent re-taking its own
    # files, not a wall against it.
    if iid == me or iid not in live:
        continue
    # SPLIT ON COMMAS TOO. The log holds both separators: the usage line for
    # this script shows spaces, but a caller who wrote --files "a.ts,b.ts"
    # recorded one comma-joined token, and 4 of the 8 most recent scope lines
    # are that shape. Splitting on whitespace alone made the whole blob ONE
    # path: NEVER_CLAIMED missed the changelog.d/ inside it (walling every
    # agent off its own fragment - the 2026-08-26 incident this inversion
    # exists to prevent, live on the real log), and is_claimed could never
    # match a comma-joined token against a real path, so the claim silently
    # protected nothing.
    keep = [p for p in re.split(r"[\s,]+", live[iid]) if p and strip(p) not in NEVER_CLAIMED]
    if not keep:
        continue
    print("CLAIM\t%s\t%s" % (iid, " ".join(keep)))
    for p in keep:
        if p not in paths:
            paths.append(p)
print("PATHS\t%s" % " ".join(paths))
' "$id" 2>/dev/null || true)
  deny=$(printf '%s\n' "$claims" | awk -F'\t' '$1=="PATHS"{print $2}' || true)
  # One line per claiming issue, so the agent can see WHO holds a file and the
  # lander can go ask them. A flat path list alone reads as an arbitrary wall.
  claim_lines=$(printf '%s\n' "$claims" \
    | awk -F'\t' '$1=="CLAIM"{printf "    %s\n        (claimed by %s)\n", $3, $2}' || true)
fi

if [ "$deny_ok" -eq 0 ]; then deny_field="(unavailable)"
elif [ -z "$deny" ]; then deny_field="(none)"
else deny_field="$deny"; fi

# --files overlapping a live claim is dispatch rule 3 — two issues in one file
# serialize, they do not run concurrently. Printed, not refused: re-dispatching
# an issue over its own predecessor's files is legitimate, and the lander is the
# one who can tell that from a genuine collision. The lift-side refusal is the
# mechanism; this is the early warning.
overlap=""
for f in $files; do
  pat=$(echo "$f" | sed 's|(own)$||; s|/\*.*$||; s|/$||')
  [ -n "$pat" ] || continue
  for d in $deny; do
    case "$d" in $pat|$pat/*) overlap="$overlap $d" ;; esac
    case "$pat" in $d|$d/*) overlap="$overlap $d" ;; esac
  done
done
if [ -n "$overlap" ]; then
  echo "dispatch: WARNING — your --files overlaps a LIVE claim by another issue:" >&2
  for o in $overlap; do echo "    $o" >&2; done
  echo "  Two issues touching one file serialize; they do not run concurrently." >&2
  echo "  Land or hand off the other agent first, or narrow this one." >&2
  echo "  scripts/handoff --lift will refuse these files when this agent returns." >&2
fi

# THE INTERROGATION FIELD (i-an-agent-that-stops-is-interrogated-before-the).
# Written into the note, BEFORE the denylist field so the existing parsers that
# read `— denylist: …` to end-of-line and `— scope: … — brief: …` are unchanged.
#
# It is what makes the gate in scripts/handoff enforce a contract the agent was
# actually given rather than one invented at landing time: a note carrying this
# field is an agent that was told, in its own brief, that it would be questioned
# before its work was lifted. Notes without it — every note written before this
# landed, and hand-done work — are reported and never refused.
interrogation_field="required"

if [ "$dry" -eq 1 ]; then
  echo "dispatch: --dry-run — all checks passed, nothing recorded. Would log:"
  echo "  DISPATCH $id — scope: $files — brief: $brief — interrogation: $interrogation_field — denylist: $deny_field"
else
  "$root/scripts/tracker" remember "DISPATCH $id — scope: $files — brief: $brief — interrogation: $interrogation_field — denylist: $deny_field" --issue "$id" >/dev/null
  # CLAIM THE ISSUE IN THE SAME ACT THAT RECORDS THE BRIEF
  # (i-scripts-dispatch-does-not-claim). Recording a DISPATCH note left status
  # at `notstarted`, so a second session running `issues.py orient` saw an
  # issue with a live agent on it as free work. That happened: two issues sat
  # notstarted while agents were editing their files. The query half was fixed
  # first (is_ready excludes progress; orient prints IN PROGRESS with a claim
  # age so a stale claim cannot hide dead work) — this is the write half.
  #
  # Only notstarted is claimed. A `done` issue being re-dispatched is a
  # correction and its status is not ours to reopen silently; `progress` is
  # already claimed. Never fatal: the brief is recorded either way, and losing
  # the dispatch over a tracker hiccup would be worse than an unclaimed issue.
  # Parse the FIRST balanced JSON object, not everything up to the last brace.
  # `tracker show` prints the issue JSON and then its notes; rindex("}") found a
  # brace inside a NOTE, json.loads raised, the shell swallowed it, and the claim
  # silently did not happen — i-network-tools-answer-inside-cont was dispatched
  # on 2026-08-17 and still read `notstarted` to every other session. The old
  # form only worked for an issue with no notes yet, which is why it looked fine.
  cur=$("$root/scripts/tracker" show "$id" 2>/dev/null \
    | python3 -c 'import json,sys
s=sys.stdin.read(); d=json.JSONDecoder()
i=s.find("{")
print(d.raw_decode(s[i:])[0].get("status","") if i>=0 else "")' 2>/dev/null || true)
  if [ "$cur" = "notstarted" ]; then
    if "$root/scripts/tracker" set "$id" status=progress >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      echo "dispatch: $id claimed — status notstarted → progress."
    else
      echo "dispatch: WARNING — could not claim $id; it still reads '$cur' to other sessions." >&2
    fi
  elif [ -n "$cur" ] && [ "$cur" != "progress" ]; then
    echo "dispatch: NOTE — $id is '$cur', not claiming it. Re-dispatching finished work is a correction; set the status yourself if that is what you mean." >&2
  fi
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AGENT'S CONTEXT FILE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A FILE AND NOT MORE TERMINAL OUTPUT. planning-docs/ is a nested, gitignored
# repo, so it does not exist inside a worktree: an agent cannot read issues.json,
# its own issue text, the notes log or any PRD (scripts/tracker exits 2 there and
# says so). Subagents are also NOT given .claude/CLAUDE.md — it is not in their
# system prompt or first message, and 140 of 152 measured worktree agents never
# opened it. The one channel that reliably lands is what the brief pastes, so
# everything is written here and the brief's first line is this file's path.
#
# WHY PER-ISSUE AND NOT A SINGLE .dispatch-context.md AT THE ROOT. A dispatch
# round briefs N agents back to back from one checkout; a fixed filename means
# agent A's file is overwritten by agent B's dispatch before A ever reads it,
# and A then reads B's issue, B's scope and B's brief as its own. That is worse
# than absent, because nothing looks wrong. One file per issue cannot collide;
# re-dispatching the same issue overwrites its own file, which is correct.
#
# WHY IT LIVES IN THE MAIN CHECKOUT. It is untracked, and `git worktree add`
# materialises a commit — untracked files are NOT carried in. Verified: after
# writing it, `git worktree add` produced a tree with no .dispatch-context/ in
# it. So the path printed in the brief is ABSOLUTE and points back at this
# checkout, which the agent can read (its worktree lives under it).
#
# NOTHING HERE IS FATAL. Same principle as the claim step: the brief is recorded
# either way. A section that could not be gathered says so, and says why.
ctxdir="$root/.dispatch-context"
ctx="$ctxdir/$id.md"

# The three carry-forward fields an incoming agent can act on, lifted from the
# HANDOFF notes earlier agents left on this issue. verified/scope-released are
# the lander's bookkeeping, not the next agent's context, so they are left out.
# Computed here because it now feeds BOTH the context file and the paste block.
carry=$("$root/scripts/tracker" notes --issue "$id" 2>/dev/null | python3 -c '
import sys, re
KEEP = ("next-agent", "found-not-fixed", "assumed")
out = []
for line in sys.stdin:
    if "HANDOFF " not in line:
        continue
    for part in line.split(" | ")[1:]:
        part = part.strip()
        k = part.split(":", 1)[0].strip().lower()
        if k in KEEP and len(part) > len(k) + 1:
            out.append((k, part.split(":", 1)[1].strip()))
for k, v in out:
    v = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", v)
    print(f"    - [{k}] {v}")
' 2>/dev/null || true)

if [ "$dry" -eq 0 ]; then
  issue_text=$("$root/scripts/tracker" show "$id" 2>&1) || issue_text="UNAVAILABLE — scripts/tracker could not be reached. It said:

$issue_text

planning-docs/ is a NESTED, GITIGNORED repo. It is absent from every agent
worktree and from any checkout that has not cloned it, so the tracker exits 2
there. Ask the dispatching session for the issue text."

  # Not capped: an id with a long history is exactly the case the audit is for.
  landed=$(git log --oneline --grep="$id" || true)
  [ -n "$landed" ] || landed="(nothing — git log --oneline --grep=$id is empty; no commit in this checkout names this issue)"

  carry_text="$carry"
  [ -n "$carry_text" ] || carry_text="(nothing recorded — no earlier HANDOFF note on this issue)"

  mkdir -p "$ctxdir"
  {
    echo "# Dispatch context — $id"
    echo
    echo "Written by scripts/dispatch on $(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ') from $root ($branch @ $head)."
    echo "READ THIS BEFORE YOU DO ANYTHING ELSE. Your worktree does not contain"
    echo "planning-docs/, so the tracker, this issue's text and the shared notes log"
    echo "are unreachable from it — scripts/tracker exits 2 in there. Everything you"
    echo "cannot look up for yourself was captured here at dispatch time."
    echo
    echo "## Files you must NOT write — CLAIMED by another agent right now"
    echo
    if [ "$deny_ok" -eq 0 ]; then
      echo "    UNAVAILABLE — scripts/tracker could not be reached, so the live-claim"
      echo "    log could not be read. Treat the advisory list below as a HARD"
      echo "    boundary instead: an unreadable claim log is not an empty one."
    elif [ -z "$deny" ]; then
      echo "    (none — no other issue holds a live claim this round)"
      echo
      echo "Every file in this repo is yours to write if the fix genuinely needs it."
    else
      echo "$claim_lines"
      echo
      echo "Every OTHER file in this repo is yours to write if the fix genuinely"
      echo "needs it. Say in HANDOFF.md's changed: line what you took beyond the"
      echo "advisory list below, so the lander reads that diff on purpose."
    fi
    echo
    echo "Derived from the shared notes log, not from a guess: an issue whose last"
    echo "event is a DISPATCH holds its scope, a HANDOFF releases it, and a claim"
    echo "by this issue's own id is not a claim against you."
    echo
    echo "### If your fix needs a CLAIMED file: STOP AND REPORT. This is a refusal,"
    echo "### not a preference."
    echo
    echo "Both recorded failures of this rule were a quiet HALF-CHANGE, not a"
    echo "refusal. An agent that finds it needs a claimed file has three options —"
    echo "write it anyway and risk clobbering a concurrent agent, ship the half it"
    echo "was allowed to write, or stop — and the record shows it takes the middle"
    echo "one every time, and reports success:"
    echo
    echo "  i-commune-s-failed-controller-turn  shipped a Go writer emitting an"
    echo "      event kind whose declaration in src/shared/types.ts it could not"
    echo "      add. Both halves individually green, the wire format broken."
    echo "  i-dist-version-stamp                shipped a janusVersion wire field"
    echo "      with no producer, because cmd/janus-bridge was outside its scope."
    echo "      'daemon:' read unstamped on every build."
    echo
    echo "Neither agent said it was blocked. The cost landed on the lander, who is"
    echo "the one context that can see both halves — which is exactly the"
    echo "serialisation the dispatch model exists to avoid. So:"
    echo
    echo "  1. STOP at the boundary. Do not write the claimed file, and do not"
    echo "     ship the half of the change that does not work without it."
    echo "  2. Leave the in-scope work UNCOMMITTED AND COHERENT — it must build"
    echo "     and test on its own. If it cannot, say so in changed: rather than"
    echo "     leaving a green half that is wrong on the wire."
    echo "  3. Put the blocked half in HANDOFF.md's found: line, naming the EXACT"
    echo "     file and what it must contain — the field, the event kind, the"
    echo "     declaration. 'needed a file outside scope' is not enough for the"
    echo "     lander to finish it."
    echo "  4. Say the same in your final report, in the same words."
    echo
    echo "WHAT ENFORCES THIS: nothing mechanical, and pretending otherwise would be"
    echo "worse than saying it. Nothing can detect a half-change from outside — the"
    echo "Go half and the TS half are each individually green, which is the whole"
    echo "failure. The mechanism is this wording plus the lander reading your"
    echo "found: line, and scripts/handoff refuses a record with no --found and no"
    echo "--next unless the lander passes --clean as a positive claim."
    echo
    echo "## What the dispatcher expected you to touch — ADVISORY, not a boundary"
    echo
    echo "    $files"
    echo
    echo "This is a prediction made before anyone read the code, by the party that"
    echo "has not read it. It is recorded for the audit trail and it is NOT the"
    echo "limit of what you may write. The enforced limit is the claimed list above."
    echo
    echo "## Your brief"
    echo
    echo "    $brief"
    echo
    echo "## You will be QUESTIONED before your work is lifted"
    echo
    echo "When you stop, you are still resumable and you still hold context nobody"
    echo "else has. The lander will put these questions to you and record your"
    echo "answers on the issue; scripts/handoff refuses to record the landing until"
    echo "it has them. Gather them AS YOU WORK — they are much harder to"
    echo "reconstruct afterwards, and a wrong answer is worse than a blank one."
    echo
    # ASKED OF scripts/handoff, not restated here. Two copies of a question set
    # drift, and the copy the agent is shown is the one that would rot silently
    # — handoff owns the questions because handoff is what refuses without them.
    qs=$("$root/scripts/handoff" "$id" --questions 2>/dev/null || true)
    if [ -n "$qs" ]; then echo "$qs"
    else echo "  (unavailable — scripts/handoff --questions could not be read here)"; fi
    echo
    echo "None of these can be answered with \"no\", and that is deliberate. Both"
    echo "recorded half-changes were reported as successes by the agent that shipped"
    echo "them, so \"are you complete?\" is worse than asking nothing. At least one"
    echo "of your answers must be substantive — \"nothing\" across the whole set is"
    echo "refused, so silence has to be a claim you made rather than an absence."
    echo
    echo "## The issue (scripts/tracker show $id — JSON, then its notes)"
    echo
    echo "$issue_text"
    echo
    echo "## What already landed for this issue (git log --oneline --grep=$id)"
    echo
    echo "IF THIS IS NON-EMPTY, AUDIT IT BEFORE WRITING CODE. An issue whose desc has"
    echo "gone stale reads as unstarted work; one run burned ~75k tokens reimplementing"
    echo "a fix that was already in the log under its own id."
    echo
    echo "$landed"
    echo
    echo "## What earlier work on this issue left you"
    echo
    echo "$carry_text"
    echo
    echo "## The repo's working rules — .claude/CLAUDE.md, VERBATIM"
    echo
    echo "You are NOT given this file automatically: it is not in your system prompt"
    echo "and not in your first message. It is inlined whole rather than summarised,"
    echo "because a hand-picked subset drifts from the real file and nothing notices."
    echo
    echo "===== BEGIN .claude/CLAUDE.md ====="
    if [ -f "$root/.claude/CLAUDE.md" ]; then
      cat "$root/.claude/CLAUDE.md"
    else
      echo "UNAVAILABLE — $root/.claude/CLAUDE.md does not exist in this checkout."
    fi
    echo "===== END .claude/CLAUDE.md ====="
    echo
    echo "## Before you finish: write HANDOFF.md at the root of YOUR worktree"
    echo
    echo "Exactly these four keys, ONE LINE each, no prose, under six lines total:"
    echo
    echo "    changed: <what you changed>"
    echo "    found:   <seen but not fixed, out of scope — or: none>"
    echo "    assumed: <what you took as given — or: none>"
    echo "    next:    <what the next agent needs to know — or: none>"
    echo
    echo "Do not commit it; it is gitignored. found and next are why the file exists"
    echo "— your diff already says what changed. Do not commit, push or merge anything"
    echo "at all: the dispatching session lifts your worktree and lands it."
  } > "$ctx"
  echo "dispatch: context written — $ctx"
fi

# The issue's own note thread, printed at the moment a brief is being written.
# `notes --issue` has existed all along and nothing ever put it in front of the
# person who needed it, so a prior agent's findings on the same issue stayed
# unread by the next one. Excludes the DISPATCH line just written.
prior=$("$root/scripts/tracker" notes --issue "$id" 2>/dev/null | grep -v "brief: $brief" || true)
if [ -n "$prior" ]; then
  echo
  echo "PRIOR NOTES ON $id — read before briefing; fold anything load-bearing into the brief:"
  echo "$prior" | sed 's/^/  /'
fi

echo
if [ "$dry" -eq 1 ]; then
  echo "dispatch: OK (dry run) — nothing recorded on $id."
else
  echo "dispatch: OK — brief + scope recorded on $id."
fi
echo "Dispatch as subagent_type: isolated-worker with:"
echo "  issue: $id"
echo "  advisory --files: $files"
echo "  ENFORCED denylist: $deny_field"
echo "  brief: $brief"
echo "Remind the agent: npm run e2e (never npm run build); changelog.d fragment"
echo "per commit; leave edits uncommitted. node_modules and planning-docs are"
echo "linked into a new worktree by scripts/git-hooks/post-checkout, so the agent"
echo "no longer has to be told to do it by hand. Hooks are PER-CLONE: if nobody"
echo "ran \`npm run hooks:install\` here, the links are absent and"
echo "scripts/preflight-tree.mjs prints the ln -s to run."
echo
# Pasted verbatim into the agent's brief. Four keys, one line each, and the
# instruction says so twice — an agent given a free-form "write up what you
# found" returns prose the lander then has to read and compress, which is the
# work this was meant to remove. The file is gitignored, so the agent cannot
# accidentally add it to the tree.
echo "PASTE INTO THE AGENT'S BRIEF:"
# FIRST LINE OF THE PASTE BLOCK, and the load-bearing one. Measured across 152
# worktree agents: a brief that PASTES an instruction gets ~96% compliance, a
# brief that POINTS AT A FILE gets 38%, and a file nobody mentions gets 8%. So
# the context file is only worth writing if its path is pasted — and it must be
# the ABSOLUTE path, because the file lives in this checkout and untracked files
# are not carried into a worktree.
echo "  FIRST, before anything else: read this file, in full, with one Read:"
echo "    $ctx"
echo "  It is your issue text, what already landed under this id, your scope and"
echo "  brief, what earlier agents left you, and this repo's working rules"
echo "  (.claude/CLAUDE.md — you are NOT given it automatically). It is in the MAIN"
echo "  checkout, NOT in your worktree; the path above is absolute, use it as-is."
if [ "$dry" -eq 1 ]; then
  echo "  (--dry-run: that file was NOT written. Rerun without --dry-run.)"
fi
# The cwd check, stated to the AGENT. This script already refuses a non-root
# cwd, but it runs when the brief is written and the Agent call happens after —
# a `cd` in between still misroutes the worktree, and on 2026-08-17 that took
# out three agents at once (worktrees of the nested planning-docs repo: no
# src/, no janus-bridge/, no package.json). Nothing in the tool chain can catch
# that on the way in, so the agent checks on the way out.
#
# IT ASKED FOR --show-toplevel UNTIL 2026-08-27, AND NO AGENT COULD EVER PASS
# IT (i-the-dispatch-brief-tells-every-agent-to-run-a). The old text was: run
# `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, it MUST end in /browser-wrap and MUST NOT
# contain /planning-docs, if it does STOP and do not write to the shared
# checkout. Every dispatched agent is in a WORKTREE, whose toplevel is
# .claude/worktrees/agent-<id> — it can never end in /browser-wrap, so the
# first half was false for every reader. The two halves also contradicted each
# other: the path ending in /browser-wrap IS the shared checkout, so read
# literally it said pass by being in the shared checkout, then stop because you
# are in the shared checkout. A literal agent either halts on a correct setup
# or cds INTO the shared tree to comply — which is the 2026-08-17 disaster this
# check exists to prevent. Reported by the worker on
# i-dispatch-declares-what-is-claimed-by-others-not (which reasoned past it by
# substituting --git-common-dir) and confirmed by a verifier sweep from inside
# its own worktree.
#
# --git-common-dir is the check that answers the question actually being asked:
# WHICH REPO IS THIS A WORKTREE OF. From a linked worktree it resolves to the
# MAIN repo's .git as an ABSOLUTE path (probed: a worktree of a repo at
# /tmp/dispatch-ctx-X reports /tmp/dispatch-ctx-X/.git), so it carries the
# repo's own path and the /planning-docs refusal keeps working verbatim. From a
# non-worktree checkout at its root it prints a bare relative `.git`, which is
# why the wording calls that out as its own reportable state rather than
# leaving the agent to interpret it.
#
# WHAT ENFORCES IT: tests/core/dispatch-context.test.ts extracts this command
# from the printed paste block, RUNS it in a real worktree, and asserts the
# result satisfies both MUSTs — and asserts in the same place that the old
# --show-toplevel check does not. A prose instruction that nothing executes is
# how the broken one survived from the day it was added.
# The denylist, IN the paste block. The measured numbers above are the whole
# argument: pasted text lands at ~96%, a pointed-at file at 38%. The context
# file carries the full stop-and-report protocol; this is the one line that has
# to survive a dispatcher who pastes and nothing else.
echo "  Your scope is a DENYLIST, not an allowlist. These files are claimed by"
echo "  another agent RIGHT NOW and you must NOT write them: $deny_field"
echo "  Everything else is yours if the fix needs it. If your fix needs a CLAIMED"
echo "  file: STOP — do not write it, and do NOT ship the half you were allowed"
echo "  to write. Leave the in-scope work coherent and name the exact file and"
echo "  what it must contain in HANDOFF.md's found: line. The context file above"
echo "  has the full protocol and says what does and does not enforce it."
echo "  SECOND, before you write anything: run \`git rev-parse --git-common-dir\`."
echo "  It prints the MAIN repo's .git — which repo your worktree branched from."
echo "  It MUST contain /$(basename "$root")/ and MUST NOT contain /planning-docs."
echo "  A bare relative \`.git\` means you are not in a worktree at all: report that."
echo "  If any of those is wrong, STOP and report it. NEVER cd to make it pass and"
echo "  never write to the shared checkout — a cd is the failure, not the fix."
echo "  Do NOT use --show-toplevel here: yours is the worktree path, so it can"
echo "  never end in /$(basename "$root") and tells you nothing about the repo."
# THE SCRATCHPAD, IN THE PASTE BLOCK RATHER THAN ONLY IN A SKILL
# (i-concurrent-agents-share-one-scratchpad-and). The worker-facing half of that
# fix could not be written when it landed, because this file was claimed by
# another agent that round; its own HANDOFF found: line asked for these lines.
# It matters where it goes: the same measurement that shapes the rest of this
# block puts a pasted rule at ~96% and one sitting in CLAUDE.md at 7-8%, and a
# scratchpad the worker never hears about is the shared one it was already using.
echo "  YOUR PRIVATE SCRATCHPAD is \`.scratch\` at the root of your worktree —"
echo "  mutators, probe harnesses, captured suite output go THERE. The session"
echo "  scratchpad is shared with every agent running beside you: on 2026-08-27"
echo "  two of them wrote m1/m2/m3 mutators into one directory nine minutes"
echo "  apart and missed each other only on the suffix. If a tool refuses to"
echo "  write through .scratch, say so in HANDOFF.md — do not fall back to the"
echo "  shared scratchpad silently."
# Earlier agents' carry-forward lines, INSIDE the paste block rather than above
# it. The agent cannot look any of this up for itself: planning-docs/ is a
# nested repo and is gitignored, so it does not exist in a worktree and neither
# scripts/tracker nor the notes log is reachable from there. Whatever is not
# pasted is, for the agent, unwritten.
#
# Until now this printed only as "PRIOR NOTES … fold anything load-bearing into
# the brief" — advice, above the block, with nothing enforcing the fold. It was
# the one link in the chain with no mechanism behind it. Now the three fields an
# incoming agent can act on (next-agent, found-not-fixed, assumed) are inside
# the text that gets pasted, and verified/scope-released are left out because
# they are the lander's bookkeeping, not the next agent's context.
if [ -n "$carry" ]; then
  echo "  WHAT EARLIER WORK ON THIS ISSUE LEFT YOU (you cannot look this up —"
  echo "  the tracker does not exist inside your worktree):"
  echo "$carry"
  echo
fi
echo "  Before you finish, write HANDOFF.md at the root of your worktree."
echo "  Exactly these four keys, ONE LINE each, no prose:"
echo "    changed: <what you changed>"
echo "    found:   <seen but not fixed, out of scope — or: none>"
echo "    assumed: <what you took as given — or: none>"
echo "    next:    <what the next agent needs to know — or: none>"
echo "  Keep it under six lines total. Do not commit it; it is gitignored."
# IN THE PASTE BLOCK, not only in the context file, for the measured reason: 96%
# compliance for what a brief pastes against 38% for what it points at. The
# questions themselves are in the context file; what has to survive a dispatcher
# who pastes and nothing else is that the questions are COMING, so the agent
# tracks the answers instead of reconstructing them.
echo "  AFTER YOU STOP YOU WILL BE QUESTIONED, while you are still resumable, and"
echo "  your work is not lifted until your answers are recorded. The questions are"
echo "  adversarial on purpose — what you did NOT run, which of your assertions is"
echo "  weakest, what you took on trust, what in the brief you skipped, what you"
echo "  would check next. They are listed in the context file above; note the"
echo "  answers as you work. \"Nothing\" to every one of them is refused."
echo
echo "WHEN YOU LAND IT, before committing:"
echo "  scripts/handoff $id --interrogate                     # the questions; send them to the LIVE agent"
echo "  scripts/handoff $id --record-interrogation --answer \"...\"   # what it said"
echo "  scripts/handoff $id --from <agent-worktree> --lift    # copies the files IN, refusing any that drifted"
echo "  ...then verify in THIS tree, then:"
echo "  scripts/handoff $id --from <agent-worktree> --verified \"what YOU re-ran\""
echo "  (or spell the fields out: --changed \"...\" [--found \"...\"] [--next \"...\"] [--clean])"
echo "  The commit-msg hook refuses a commit naming $id until that exists."
